   "round": "complete-recipe (cited+supplementary chased)",
   "status": "corrected",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources_chased": [
    {
     "what": "base recipe cited as ref 70 (Elbing & Brent 2002, 5x M63 salts)",
     "obtained": true,
     "how": "WebSearch of Elbing & Brent 2002 Current Protocols M63 recipe (Wiley DOI 10.1002/0471142727.mb0101s59 returned 403; recipe confirmed via multiple protocol mirrors: protocolsonline, laboratorynotes, PMC6819147)",
     "added": [
      "(NH4)2SO4 2 g/L",
      "KH2PO4 13.6 g/L",
      "FeSO4.7H2O 0.5 mg/L",
      "KOH pH 7",
      "explicit concentrations for all salts"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "missing_before": [
    "identity/concentration of base salts: (NH4)2SO4 (2 g/L), KH2PO4 (13.6 g/L)",
    "FeSO4.7H2O 0.5 mg/L (iron was present as EX_fe2_e but no source/conc)",
    "KOH (pH 7 titrant)",
    "ampicillin 100 ug/mL (selection antibiotic)"
   ],
   "corrections": [],
   "notes": "M63+glu is a fully defined minimal medium; the paper gives only supplements (B1, MgSO4, glucose) and cites ref 70 for the 5x M63 salt base, which supplies ammonium, phosphate/K buffer, and the sole trace metal (FeSO4). M63 by definition contains no additional trace-element mix and thiamine is its only vitamin, so the recipe is complete. The GEM exchange list already covered every ion; this pass recovers compound identities, exact concentrations, KOH and ampicillin."
  },
